  * 5000 ms is 10x the measured 0.501 s A tail. The retry path (0593)
  * re-sends only the final 183-byte frame. B/C and every other phase keep
  * the shared strict reader and v2060's bytes. */
+
+/* Level 20.67 (v2064, 0602): map A is a stream, not an ACK exchange.
+ *
+ * 0601 kept a 5000 ms sub-06 gate on the 284-frame map A stage and drained the
+ * tail for 500 ms before asking 0x19. This ROM does answer sub-06 ACK for the
+ * level-19 RAM patch and NVM stages (two 12-byte records at 0.929 s and
+ * 0.947 s), so the reader is right; it simply never does for map A, and instead
+ * starts its resident 34 00 ff fc f0 04 readback 171 ms after the last byte at
+ * 144.5 ms per record. The Android trace sends 0x19 54.1 ms after the same
+ * point. The window is therefore tens of milliseconds wide: every wait before
+ * 0x19 is spent inside a window that is closing.
+ *
+ * 0602 sends map A with ack_ms = 0 (SKIP_EVT_VSE_CC, the reference HAL's own
+ * semantics), timestamps the last byte, and asks 0x19 at once with 250 ms then
+ * 1500 ms. Silence is recorded as a rejection of map A with the resident
+ * stream's seq progress and map B is withheld. A reset is never part of the
+ * accept path: pandora_bt_reset=2 runs it only after a silent 0x19 with a BID
+ * answer, and it always replays the whole 284-frame map A before asking 0x19
+ * again. "reset -> 0x19 answered -> map A accepted" is not a valid triple.
+ */
+#define BT_PERI_MAP_A_0X19_FIRST_MS 250u
+#define BT_PERI_MAP_A_0X19_WIDE_MS  1500u
+#define BT_PERI_MAP_A_TAIL_SCAN_MS  150u
+#define BT_PERI_MAP_A_WITHHOLD      0x0c02
+
+static uint64_t bt_map_a_last_tx;
+static int bt_map_a_reset_arm;
+
 #define BT_PERI_ASEG_LAST_ACK_MS 5000u
 /* Level 20.64 (v2061, 0593): after a no-ACK timeout, re-send only
  * the final frame and wait again. Two retries = three waits; an
@@ -4684,6 +4712,9 @@
